[Event "French: Advance Variation"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e6 {The French: first ...e6, then ...d5. Solid, and you fight for the center with pawns.} 2. d4 d5 3. e5 {White grabs space. Now the plan of every French player: attack White's pawn chain from the side.} c5 {Hit d4, the base of the chain.} 4. c3 Nc6 {More pressure on d4.} 5. Nf3 Qb6 {The queen joins the attack on d4 and looks at b2.} 6. a3 Nge7 {The knight goes to f5 or g6 – and still attacks d4.} 7. dxc5 Qc7 {Hit e5: White's center starts to wobble.} 8. b4 Ng6 *

[Event "French: Exchange Variation"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 {Symmetrical and quiet. Your light bishop is free now.} 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bd3 c5 {Do not just copy White: ...c5 makes the game yours.} 6. c3 Be7 {Develop and castle soon.} 7. dxc5 Bxc5 *

[Event "French: Tarrasch 3.Nd2"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 {The Tarrasch: the knight does not block the c-pawn.} c5 {Strike at d4 at once.} 4. exd5 exd5 5. Bb5+ Bd7 {Block the check with a piece that wants to develop anyway.} 6. Qe2+ Be7 {Block again – then castle.} 7. dxc5 Nf6 *

[Event "French: Steinitz 4.e5"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {Attack e4.} 4. e5 Nfd7 {The knight steps back – and goes on to attack e5 or b6.} 5. f4 c5 {Again: hit the pawn chain at d4.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 {Develop, castle, then ...a6 and ...b5 on the queenside.} 8. Qd2 a6 *

[Event "King's Indian: Classical"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 {The King's Indian: you let White build a big center – and attack it later.} 3. Nc3 Bg7 {Your bishop on g7 is the heart of the King's Indian.} 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O {King safe first.} 6. Be2 e5 {Now strike at the center.} 7. O-O Nc6 {Pressure on d4.} 8. d5 Ne7 {White closes the center: your knight goes to the kingside, where your attack will come with ...f5.} 9. b4 a5 *

[Event "King's Indian vs the London"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 {The same setup against the London: bishop to g7, castle.} 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. c4 c5 {Hit d4 – the London is weak against ...c5.} 6. Nc3 cxd4 7. exd4 d5 *

[Event "King's Indian: Fianchetto"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 {Prepare ...e5.} 7. Nc3 e5 {The King's Indian strike in the center.} 8. e4 Re8 {Support e5 – and the rook watches the e-file.} *

[Event "Slav: Main Line 4...dxc4"]
[Opening "Slav Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 {The Slav: protect d5 with the c-pawn – your light bishop stays free.} 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 {Take the pawn: White must spend time to win it back.} 5. a4 Bf5 {Your bishop comes out before ...e6 – the big idea of the Slav.} 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 {Pin the knight and castle.} 8. O-O O-O *

[Event "Slav: Exchange Variation"]
[Opening "Slav Defense"]
[Side "Black"]
[Result "*"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 cxd5 {Symmetrical. Develop well and do not fall behind.} 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bf4 Nc6 {Pressure on d4.} 6. e3 Bf5 {Your bishop comes out before ...e6, as always in the Slav.} 7. Qb3 Na5 {Chase the queen away from b7.} *

